8a0d2738b28015`\n- Files changed: 33 tiered-serve files + 2 vLLM integration files\n\n## Startup order\n\n1. `serving_max_batched_tokens.patch` (or its `from_1024` variant)\n2. `serving_performance_suite.patch`\n3. `apply_cd3.sh /root/projects/tiered-serve`\n\n`apply_cd3.sh` verifies and idempotently applies `cd3_current.patch` plus\n`cd3_vllm_integration.patch`. The latter teaches the KV planner CD3's fixed\ncompressed-row size and reserves resident partial-tail rows in full-block\nmode. The obsolete two-stage CPU/KV patch was removed after verifying that all\nof its paths are contained in the consolidated patch.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis snapshot contains the current CD3 weight, fused-GEMM, KV codec, CPU\nweight/KV encoders, benchmark, serving integration, model configuration, and\nCD3 tests. Weight encode defaults to CPU; CUDA is used only to stage completed\ncompressed buffers and for decode/fused-MM. The legacy GPU encoder is available\nonly through the explicit debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\nUnrelated working-tree changes were intentionally excluded.\n\nThe CPU KV encoder additionally contains the 2026-09-01 optimization set:\n\n- block-owner, single-source-pass encoding;\n- min/max exception-free fast path;\n- SVE2 predicate counting on the exception path;\n- NEON hierarchical packing of 16 three-bit codes;\n- uninitialized temporary exception scratch.\n\nDirect-to-persistent Packed20 packing was measured but not retained because it\nwas 3–6% slower for medium and large batches.\n\nThe consolidated snapshot additionally contains:\n\n- host-native CD3 weight encoding with 64-row stripe parallelism;\n- adaptive OpenMP team sizing for a single KV compression worker;\n- batched NumPy KV slot allocation instead of a per-block Python loop;\n- production fused-MM crossover calibration through N=128 on GH200.\n- 128-thread standalone CD3 decode, with two logical chunks per thread;\n- two half-width live output accumulators with the same two 128-bit stores,\n  reducing standalone decode latency without changing the format;\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV decode launch geometry;\n- direct-to-cache indexed KV decode with no raw-BF16 intermediate copy;\n- selected-run KV staging for partial restores below the slot high-water mark.\n- one aligned slot-major CD3 blob and row-stride-aware CPU/GPU kernels;\n- one `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all K/V staging runs;\n- cached block/slot device metadata and one native K/V decode dispatch.\n- active-set identity/content caching with generation-safe invalidation;\n- a dense steady-state decode plan that skips filtering, sorting and run scans.\n- once-per-forward active-set publication instead of repeated per-layer NumPy\n  comparisons;\n- explicit-stream native paired decode dispatch;\n- a two-slot compressed-blob HBM ring that overlaps layer L+1 H2D with layer L\n  decode for N>=256, while preserving the cheaper one-stream path below it;\n- serving-suite codec selection through `--codecs p4flat,cd3`, including\n  codec-specific weight/KV arguments, configs, result labels and manifests;\n- a fully CPU P4Flat weight post-process (blocked relayout and subtile codec),\n  so CPU weight encoding no longer launches CUDA encode kernels;\n- a frozen-split weight benchmark that separately reports H2D, resident GPU\n  decode, and their combined latency.\n- projection-specific fused-N calibration overrides, with a uniform override\n  retained for compatibility;\n- exact-length CD3 frozen-split staging views, so standalone fallback accepts\n  variable-sized Packed20x3 exception streams;\n- startup route reporting that reflects projection-specific overrides.\n- a GH200/Llama-70B prefill weight-stream cutoff of N=1856: compressed\n  standalone decode below the cutoff and raw BF16 transfer at or above it;\n- an AST-level regression test that pins the calibrated CD3 cutoff.\n\n## Validation\n\n- The old CD3 patch's complete path set is contained in this patch.\n- Applied cleanly after startup prerequisites on source HEAD.\n- All 33 restored tiered-serve files matched the selected live files\n  byte-for-byte.\n- `git diff --check`, Python byte compilation, and shell syntax checks passed.\n- The consolidated patch applied after both startup prerequisites in a clean\n  worktree and all 33 restored files matched the live files byte-for-byte.\n- The vLLM integration patch reverse-checks against the validated live tree.\n- 34 CD3, prefill weight-stream, and KV-prefill policy tests passed.\n- Actual Llama-3.1-70B weight round-trip: qkv/o/gateup/down all bit-exact.\n- Layer-40 CD3 CPU weight encode median total: 130.7 ms, versus P4Flat CPU\n  1951.9 ms (about 14.9x faster); compression ratio about 1.368x versus 1.326x.\n- GH200 CPU KV encode median with one serving worker, K+V: N=8 0.16 ms;\n  N=64 0.27 ms; N=128 0.37 ms; N=256 0.47 ms; N=1024 0.99 ms;\n  N=2048 2.02 ms. All cases were bit-exact.\n- Full serving initialization installed all 320 fused Linear overrides and\n  completed all 320 CPU-compressed weight cells.\n- Layer-40 standalone weight decode median gained another 1.4% in aggregate\n  from half-width live output accumulation (all four groups bit-exact).\n- Resident production KV decode improved 1.69x--2.04x for N=8/64/256 by\n  writing directly to the final cache. Partial H2D restore at high-water=1024\n  improved 16% for N=64 and 12% for N=256.\n- Final current-stride standalone K+V H2D+decode medians for CD3 are\n  0.043/0.041/0.079/0.223 ms at N=8/64/256/1024 versus P4Flat\n  0.062/0.065/0.092/0.247 ms, making CD3 30.6/36.0/13.8/9.8% faster.\n- A 16-layer CD3 pipeline gains 30.3% at N=256 and 25.8% at N=1024 from\n  H2D/decode overlap; all cases remained bit-exact.\n- CPU-encoded production KV H2D+decode steady-state median on GH200 is\n  0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for N=8/64/256/1024. P4Flat under the same\n  split-measurement run is 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms. CD3 resident decode is\n  0.028/0.029/0.036/0.065 ms versus P4Flat 0.046/0.047/0.049/0.082 ms; CD3\n  H2D is 0.016/0.026/0.056/0.173 ms versus 0.030/0.042/0.081/0.232 ms.\n  The pre-blob CD3 values were\n  0.247/0.291/0.374/0.762 ms; all cases were bit-exact. This historical P4Flat\n  comparison used its raw-safe 32,768-byte blob stride. Current P4Flat defaults\n  to a 25,600-byte tight stride, so these P4Flat values must not be used as the\n  final current-codec comparison.\n- Layer-40 frozen-split weight medians on GH200 (qkv/o/gateup/down summed):\n  CD3 H2D 1.200 ms, resident decode 0.923 ms, H2D+decode 2.127 ms; P4Flat\n  H2D 1.353 ms, resident decode 0.906 ms, H2D+decode 2.232 ms. CD3 therefore\n  transfers 11.3% faster and finishes the production pair 4.7% faster while\n  its isolated decode is 1.9% slower. All four weights were bit-exact.\n- Rejected experiments are not present: warp-owner exception pre-application\n  was about 2.3x slower, pairwise 3xU32 code loading was neutral/regressive,\n  64-thread or register-forced 10-CTA kernels were 7--12% slower, and a\n  separate standalone exception kernel was 50.7% slower than integrated patch.\n- Fused route calibration with concurrent H2D found all four projections faster\n  for every tested N=65..128. At N=129 the Npad=192 transition makes fused\n  about 1.6--1.7x slower, so the production cutoff is exactly 128.\n- An all-80-layer QKV sweep found fused wins on 79/80 layers and saves\n  0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128. The exception-heavy layer\n  0 is the sole loser. End-to-end TPOT P50 for all-fused versus QKV-standalone\n  was 171.90/188.40/219.26 versus 172.20/187.96/219.30 ms at batch\n  80/96/128, confirming a tie at engine level and favoring all-fused because it\n  avoids dense-weight HBM writes.\n- End-to-end forced-route prefill sweeps placed the standalone/raw crossover\n  between N=1824 and N=1856. At N=1824 standalone led by 18.630 ms; at N=1856\n  raw led by 20.405 ms, and raw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run\n  confirmed standalone routing